message.content:
{
  "answer_markdown": "To tell which version an alias currently points at, you can refer to the [model capability matrix](https://docs.mistral.ai/models), which lists all available models and their corresponding API names, status, and release dates. For example, the alias `mistral-medium-latest` points to `mistral-medium-3-5` as of the latest General Availability (GA) version [3].\n\nAliases automatically switch to newer models once they reach General Availability. Therefore, the version an alias currently points to may change when a new GA model is released [1].\n\nThere is no explicit information on the schedule or conditions for when aliases will change, other than the fact that they update upon reaching GA status [1].\n\n```\n# Example of checking the current version of an alias\ncurl -X GET \"https://docs.mistral.ai/models\"\n```\n\nIf you need to pin to a specific version and avoid silent updates, use the fixed major.minor version identifier, such as `mistral-medium-3-5` instead of `mistral-medium-latest` [1].",
  "citations": [
    {
      "n": 1,
      "quote": "Aliases automatically switch to newer models once they reach General Availability. As a result, using an alias may expose you to silent updates in model behavior and pricing. For precise control, pin your deployment to a specific major.minor version identifier."
    },
    {
      "n": 3,
      "quote": "Model | API names | Status | Context | Release date |n | --- | --- | --- | --- |n | [Mistral Medium 3.5](https://docs.mistral.ai/models/mistral-medium-3-5-26-04) | `mistral-medium-3-5`, `mistral-medium-3`, `mistral-medium-latest` | GA | 256k | 2026-04-28 |"
    }
  ],
  "insufficient_evidence": false
}

